capable of and making sure  that it's sort of pervasive across roles  it's really important they want a great  battery life of course and a touchscreen  was actually close to the top four top  four reasons that we got from Googlers  and so achieving all of these things was  actually quite simple for us with pixel  book because it is high performance it  is a high quality piece of hardware it  has a touchscreen and it can work in  tablet mode and it's the lightest device  in the fleet it's so because of that the  employees that we survey 90 percent of  them are quite satisfied and want to  keep and would recommend their pixel  book to another team member and so once  you've selected the kind of devices that  you're gonna bring in your environment  now the next question is more strategic  are we going to mandate from a business  standpoint that users will now adopt  Chromebooks that's our new fleet or is  there going to be an ecosystem of choice  where people can choose and you need to  influence their behavior and so at  Google we actually do have both  scenarios and different teams for  different reasons and so each of those  different approaches require different  strategic ways of executing on them  managing change and telling people that  their platform preference is not going  to be part of the story when they select  their device requires things like making  sure that you're choosing the right  people to start with and expanding your  scope  two better candidates and if you saw our  presentation last year and it's up on  the channel you can reference it if you  did it we talked a lot about  segmentation and how we think about  segmenting user types and identifying  good candidates for Chrome OS before we  sort of push the device in mass to  people who might not be prepared for it  and what you'll learn later in this  presentation is that the percentage of  people who don't have experience with  Chrome OS is actually a lot higher than  even we anticipated within Google and so  that means that if you're going to push  this change it requires a lot of care  and if you're working in an ecosystem  where people have a choice that's a  different story and so that's where we  do a lot of marketing initiatives what  we found is that getting the device in  the hands of the users is one of the  most effective ways to get them excited  about the device and so Russ is going to  talk later about grab-and-go which is  one of the key initiatives that help  users get experience with Chrome OS  before they sort of make the plunge or  or exposed to the change and so I call  this out because if you're going to  deploy Chromebooks you have to sort of  consider which of these if not both are  going to be true for you and then  consider that either change management  or a lot of Education and marketing is  going to be required depending on what  you do obviously from a provisioning  standpoint we're really excited that  chrome so easy to provision so in the  case of a Chromebook it's really a  matter of enterprise and rolling the  device and delivering it to the desk  because of the nature of the way that we  image alternative platforms like Mac and  Windows  we save a hundred and nine minutes every  time a Chromebook has deployed a place  of one of those other platforms and so  from a management and operation  standpoint the more Chromebooks we  deploy we have this sort of exponential  gain and that's just based on the nature  of images for those other platforms  within our ecosystem and so you've  provisioned a device which is great and  you've delivered it to the users desk  and this is where we actually learned  quite a lot and this whole presentation  is designed to try to prevent you from  having to learn all of the things that  we spent a long time figuring out and so  what we first tried to do was just  respect that people were new to the  platform and then give them as much  information as possible for them to be  successful  and the way that we sort of determine  its success at this point which is  getting up and running and sort of  keeping the Chromebook as your primary  device is do they stay on the platform  and so if users if we're experiencing  attrition from the platform that means  we're doing something wrong  and so what we learned is there's sort  of three basic phases that users are  going through when they're adopting this  new platform the first and foremost  thing on their mind especially with new  hires is I just want to get email  calendar and like the essential work  apps set up that's the first thing on  their mind so when we talk about  sophisticated like cool pro tips and  productivity hacks and workflow examples  it's way too much it actually underlines  that they're about to experience a  learning curve and so we don't present  that mountain on the first day that  somebody gets a Chromebook we sort of  ease them into the basic critical things  that they need the kind of second phase  after the first few days and once you  get into that first or second week is  personalization so people tend to  download personal apps they're starting  to translate their workflows from other  platforms and so that's sort of like  basic information of tips and tricks I  used to do this on Windows how do I do  it on Chrome or I'm more familiar with  Mac kind of curated information that  will help people ease into the  transition is what we present at that  time frame and then once you get a few  weeks further down the line that's when  we get into that productivity hacks  here's I use hotkeys and keyboard  shortcuts and those type of things and  so this is an important lesson for us  because eighty-four percent of the  Googlers that were assigned a Chromebook  device this year had little to zero use  of the platform and that's a lot that's  self-reported when we survey the new  people who were assigned this year and  so that really taught us a lot about the  type of information that we need to  provide and how we need to break it down  and so again the artifacts that we use  and the strategy will be given to you  guys at the end of this presentation so  including tips and tricks campaigns and  the type of content we have there and  also surveys so we do survey all new  users of Chrome OS and we asked them  about their initial impressions so that  we can give direction for additional  resources that they need but another  important part of running Chromebook  adoption is staying on top of sort of a  monitoring goal so if you set a target  for Chromebook adoption or you're just  trying to understand the basic health  when you introduce this in the  environment there's a massive monitoring  and dashboard need and we spent so many  iterations design  charts and figuring out what questions  to ask should this be a part chart  should this be overlaid on something  else and so we've done all of that work  and provided a sort of template example  for you on what a dashboard could look  like if you're trying to answer some of  these questions because these are the  five things that we generally wanted to  answer how many people have a Chromebook  versus how many people are using it as  their actual daily drivers so there is a  circumstance where an employee could  potentially have more than one device  and we treat that circumstance  differently so if they have a Chromebook  and also another platform that's not the  same definition of success as if a  Chromebook is their only operating  system and so that's question number one  for us in terms of defining adoption we  also want to know why people leave  Chrome OS and you could just ask them  and you'll get this massive flow of open  text that you have to later interpret  we've also done that for you so we've  given you sort of a list of reasons that  you can use to aggregate and collect  that data how many people are choosing  the laptop at the time that they have a  hardware decision so any milestone where  people could select an alternative  platform whether it's moving to a new  role or refreshing a device we want to  know what the trends are their new hire  assignment is absolutely critical  because it sort of sets the tone for  what the future will look like in terms  of growth if you're trying to kind of  create a new population of users versus  focusing on converting the current  population and then lastly where does  Chrome assignment stand against some of  the other platforms so our goal is that  Chrome assignments are above the other  platforms and obviously in this sort of  up into the right line so we've created  some examples on those questions and how  we track him monitor them so that you  can potentially take that back and so I  mentioned that we serve a new users but  we also survey this like super important  and very well-respected group at Google  which are people who leave the platform  and so they tell one of the most  important data parts of the story what  we found is that you could ask a user  hey if we gave you a Chromebook would  you be able to use it and you'll get a  lot of anecdotal and hypothetical  reasons why they could or could not but  actually asking someone to try it and  figuring out when they leave the  platform in their journey and why  they're leaving produces the most  valuable insights for us and so what we  learned is that 30% of the people who  left the platform left for reasons that  we could  in theory control and so reason category  is like education resources training  materials communication about what the  platform is and what it does and so  because of that that attrition data both  tells us how to set the direction to  sort of maintain retention but also  gives us the most actionable feedback  for product teams and we'll talk a  little bit about that right now so our  goal is obviously that people can use  the platform as much as possible but we  also have this responsibility to  communicate this story of if people  can't use it or don't want to let's  figure out why and make chrome  competitive against the other platforms  and so we encourage all of our customers  to sort of do the same filing a chrome  bug is sort of step one to collecting  feedback and Google does this as well  and then stack raking and prioritizing  your requests is the next phase and so  we presented a template for you in sort  of our kit that you'll get at the end of  the day and these might seem like  obvious questions but this helps sort of  narrow exactly what's required for you  to both collect and monitor your own  story but also creates an artifact that  like your Technical Account Manager or  customer success manager can help  navigate and escalate where necessary  and so this is driven by that attrition  feedback and also the new user feedback  creating those surveys in a way that's  easy to monitor and measure and present  up on a dashboard can be hard it  requires a lot of data aggregation and  we dedicated a huge amount of UX  research resources to this so that we  can present these artifacts in a way  that we think are intelligent and are  actually valid research methods so  you'll get those too and so just to sort  of summarize where we sit in terms of  Chromebook at Google  every time we deploy a Chromebook based  on the devices that we're purchasing we  save 50 percent just in device costs  every time we deploy a Chromebook over  another platform which I mentioned  before we save a hundred and nine  minutes and for every single Chrome OS  support interaction there are three from  another platform at Google and so that  means those other platforms are  generating three times as much support  and there's a lot of reasons for that  and we could talk about them but  obviously they have to do with  complexity of the platform and the way  the images work and a number of other  things that users can experience just  based on what the platform is capable of  and we do have approaching 90 thousand  active chrome devices at Google and I  mentioned before that ninety percent of  our users who have pixel books are  satisfied with the devices and would  recommend them to their employees and so  based on this sort of timeline and all  of the things that we've accomplished we  see this as great success we tripled our  adoption population since last year with  adoption being defined as users using  the platform as their primary device and  so this URL is available you guys can  also get access to this after the  session but what this URL points to is  the timeline that we spoke about and all  of the artifacts that are related to  each of those milestones so that  includes surveys tips and tricks'  campaigns the dashboard template example  and any other resources that we felt  like could be beneficial to you wherever  you are in the stage of your adoption  journey so definitely take a snapshot of  that so you can download it and have a  look and so I mentioned before that one  of the best ways to get users excited  about the platform is to get the device  in the hands of the user and grab-and-go  has been one of our most successful ways  to do that so Russ is here to talk about  that great thanks Francis I'm Russ let  me talk to a little bit about  grab-and-go our self-service Chromebook  loaner program that we run at Google  we're gonna talk a little bit about why  we created it while we're still working  on it how it works what it is and then  talk a little bit about some of the  successes that we've seen at Google and  perhaps how you can make some of those  your own we have some really great  options for how you might be able to  deploy your own grab-and-go program so  first up let's talk about why the Google  is just like any other company we lose a  lot of time to IT downtime our devices  break just like everyone else's and when  we first started this program we were  focused on minimizing that downtime so  IHS market estimates that North American  businesses lose over 700 billion dollars  a year just to IT downtime and we also  knew that the the traditional loaner  program journey really isn't great for  anybody so are you  had to come to help desk on the help  desk was open they had to wait in line  they had to get a loaner device that  probably wasn't a great device and they  had to do that again when they were  returning it for our IT staff they were  maintaining those devices ordering them  provisioning them and they also had to  spend all the time handing them out and  none of that is really fun work for our  team so we had a lot of signals telling  us go in this direction we also had a  couple of opportunities that we knew  about things like that the fact that  Google employees use our help desks at  least once per year on average and often  times quite quite a bit more than that  and we also knew that around 80 or 85  percent of Googlers could be fully  productive on Chrome OS not all of them  required a Windows or a Mac OS to be  productive so we knew that we could put  a device in their hands that they could  get their work done on and then why  we're continuing to work on this program  we're still iterating on it we're still  making it better around 35% of Googlers  used the grab-and-go program in 2018  around half of them 18% were using the  program to work around a true  productivity blocking problem so some IT  problem maybe their device was broken or  forgotten or something like that and  then the other half another 18% or so  we're using the program for more  convenience reasons they'd be picking up  a device between meetings or people are  traveling and just want to pick up a  laptop when they arrive something like  that  so at its simplest this is grab and go  you walk up to one of our shelves there  they're just out there you can use it  whenever you'd like you walk up pick up  a device sign in with your Google  account and get back to work when you're  done you bring it back to plug it in and  that's it there's no IT team  intervention required you don't have to  talk to anybody you just walk up and  take a laptop so the core components of  the program start with the kind of  physical domain so the devices are a key  key part of this we make sure that our  devices that we put in our program are  enterprise-grade we want Googlers to be  productive when they're using devices we  want them to be durable they need to  kind of survive going from person to  person being thrown back on the shelf  being thrown in your bag and we also  want it to be cost conscious we don't  want to spend so much money that we're  really giving everyone two devices we  want it to be perhaps a little bit  cheaper than that so that's the first  piece the second is  the shelves that we buy so we we prefer  to have shelves that are modular because  we have offices of different sizes  sometimes we need a little tiny one  sometimes we need a very big one in in  all of these cases we want charging to  be built in that's a key part of the  experience you need to be able to pick  up a device and walk away with it and  know that it's fully charged so that  needs to be an obvious feature of the  shelf and we also want them to have key  safety features and that's something  that we didn't think about a lot early  on but we really do a lot now we want  them to be resistant to tipping we want  them to be grounded we want them to be  coded at a non-conductive material so  that you can kind of remove that from  from your line of thinking and the last  piece is we we designed our signs and  stickers to be very self-explanatory we  found that the the loner flow that we  designed was a little bit awkward for  some users it felt like you were walking  up and stealing a laptop if it wasn't  explained at all so the signs that we  put up there are very clear very concise  there step signs that's actually very  similar to what you saw in the last sign  saying no just really pick it up sign in  and bring it back when you're done and  we also placed instructions at key  points in the user journey so when you  open the laptop there are stickers  inside the laptop that explain how to  sign in how to connect to Wi-Fi how to  return it things like that another key  ingredient of our program is Chrome OS  itself Chrome OS makes it very easy to  just sign in and get back to work so  things like chrome sync or the  integrations with G suite products make  it incredibly easy to get back to work  once you've signed in these devices are  very very secure because of the built-in  features of chrome OS things like  verified boot automatic updates signing  restriction those are all super easy to  configure we'll talk a little bit more  about that in the next couple slides and  these devices are very very easy to  maintain we manage our entire fleet just  with the click of a few buttons and the  admin API the admin panel so we have a  very easy time managing a fleet of you  know upwards of 2,000 devices so it's  not all just signs and devices in Chrome  OS there is actually quite a large  management infrastructure that we've  built at the center of all of it is an  app engine application that's kind of  the brain of the program  that application is where program  administrators monitor the the  performance of the program who's  assigned to what which shelves might be  low in stock those kinds of things and  it also streaming all of that data and  information to bigquery another key  piece of it is Google's admin API that's  where we enforce a lot of those policies  like verified boot one other key policy  that we enforced there is a custom-built  Chrome application is pushed to every  single grab-and-go device so when the  user signs in the first thing they see  is that application popping up and  greeting them and saying hey welcome to  grab-and-go here's how you use this  device that application also guides them  through setting and return dates they  can ask questions there they can report  problems those kinds of things and then  of course all of these things allow for  integration with the other internal  tools right so we have a ticketing  system we have an inventory management  system those kinds of things so like I  said when we first deployed grab-and-go  we were doing so for the you know avoid  lost productivity situation so forgotten  broken laptops but after we deployed it  we found that Googlers were using this  program for things that we didn't really  expect and we're still not exactly sure  how to measure the you know the benefit  of those things so some of those things  are travel so we found that a lot of  Googlers will actually leave their  laptop at home travel to another office  pick up a laptop there use that put it  back on the shelf and then travel back  home just to avoid carrying a laptop  with them we also have big groups of  shift workers inside of Google we  recently published a blog on how way Moe  is using grab-and-go so they have shift  workers testing driverless cars and they  take a grab-and-go device with them  every time they go out so there's a  couple shifts of those workers and  they've saved money on not needing to  buy devices for all of them and they  also have a really great management  solution with grab-and-go and then of  course like francis was saying it's a  really great way to get Chromebooks in  people's hands using the device is a  really great way to break down barriers  and understand the power and ease of use  of Chrome OS so we're very happy with  grab-and-go Google we found that  each loan really only needs to save  about 25 minutes of productivity time to  pay for itself and our most conservative  estimates show that in 2018 we saved  around 17 million dollars if you convert  productivity time to dollar so this is  definitely something we're gonna  continue investing in and it's something  that like I said we're very happy with  if you're thinking about to point your  own grab-and-go program but you're  seeing all of these pieces that need to  be put in place and thinking I will not  do that good news we have really great  partners that can work with you so we've  spent the last year so working with some  of Google's closest partners the list is  much longer than than just what you see  here the their services range from  anywhere from from you know procuring  Hardware setting up devices customizing  the application that we built doing  inventory management training of your  team on how to run the program  anything up to and including even White  Glove service if you're more of a DIY er  and you're thinking this looks great I  can do that  that's excellent we have code all open  sourced on github github calm slash  Google slash loaner everything's there  from code to art to deployment plans to  whatever everything you'd need to deploy  your own grab-and-go program and like I  said the partners talk to your partner  definitely start a conversation there or  feel free to engage with one of the ones  that we showed on the last slide all  right well thank you everyone for coming  and thank you again have a great day  [Applause]  [Music]
 

